 :parity
 {:harness "q9-oracle.cljs"
  :command "nbb --classpath src:<kotoba-lang/text>/src q9-oracle.cljs"
  :added "2026-09-10"
  :closes "the :not-claimed item that said the oracle-* functions were RUN but never compared value-by-value"
  :agrees
  {:route-count "30 on both sides, derived from the entity table rather than written down"
   :as-int "5 of 6 selectors agree"
   :as-bool "9 of 9 selectors agree"}
  :disagrees
  {:as-int-trailing-garbage
   {:input "12abc"
    :artifact 0
    :oracle-under-nbb 12
    :oracle-under-jvm 0
    :reading
    "This is NOT a port defect, and the harness is what made that visible. The
     oracle's own as-int is a reader conditional whose two branches disagree
     with each other on this input: the :cljs branch is js/parseInt, which takes
     the leading digits and yields 12, and the :clj branch is Long/parseLong,
     which throws and is caught, yielding 0. Both were RUN rather than read --
     nbb gave 12, `clojure -M -e` gave 0.

     The component matches the :clj branch. So `parity with the oracle` is not
     well defined for this input until someone says which branch is normative,
     and a harness run under nbb will report a mismatch while the same harness
     on the JVM reports a match. Recorded rather than resolved: choosing a
     branch is a semantic decision about the product, not a migration step."}}
  :method-note
  "The expand layer is compared as a COUNT of the keys the fold added, not as a
   hash. The .kotoba hashes a rendered record, and the rendering is this port's
   own convention, so a hash comparison there would confound a behaviour
   difference with a layout difference."}

